export const summaries11to20: Record<number, { zh: string; en: string }> = {
  11: {
    zh: `徐子云送客回宅，与妻袁绮香谈起梅子玉与琴言的缱绻。袁绮香却问：这些相公究竟比自家丫鬟好在何处？子云答得冠冕：他们"面有女容，身无女体"，可以娱目又可以制心，使人有欢乐而无欲念；且外间酒席不便携女眷，正是两全其美。妻笑他说得漂亮，他自称生平不说违心话，又举白日里梅子玉被玉龄戏谑几句便怒得连自己都骂，以为佐证。次日是华夫人苏浣香二十岁正寿，袁绮香先遣人送去玉连环、翠羽扇、珊瑚搔头、青瑶琴、沉水香瑟柱、奇楠香串等十样重宝，随后带六个"红"字丫鬟乘香车赴宴。作者于此细写华府：华光宿年方二十一，气焰虽豪而性情纯粹，骑射既精，词赋更妙；夫人苏浣香乃兵部尚书之女，与其妹苏浣兰并称"侯门大小苏"，外间有"不愿得龙宫十斛珠，只愿一见侯门大小苏"之谣；府中十个美婢名字皆带"珠"字，年皆十五六岁，针黹夺天工，词令成妙品；又有八龄班小戏子，名字皆带"龄"字。是日苏浣兰先至，袁绮香继来，华公子拜过家庙便回避出去。三位女眷卧房中设席，袁绮香提出一个新酒令：掷六骰成色样，以骨牌名起句，次用五言唐诗，次用《西厢》曲文，次用曲牌名，末用《毛诗》，五句须一韵到底。于是"群鸦噪风""铁索缆孤舟""将军挂印""落红满地""二士入桃源""梅梢月上"次第掷出，三人各逞才思，或旖旎，或雄放，或温秀，彼此贺酒相赞，又互相打趣：华夫人笑袁绮香是怨丈夫日日携相公醉归，袁绮香便反笑她想起新婚燕尔的光景。袁绮香又极称通政司之女王琼华，说她十七岁便顷刻成《灯月词》七八百字，光怪陆离，恰似太白复生。后房里十珠与六红也掷骰行令，年貌相当而各怀好胜，代人捉刀之际句句藏刺，笑闹推挤，掀翻酒杯，直闹到两位夫人遣人查问。两位夫人本欲正色责备，听婢女们把六个骂人的令一一背出，反笑得珠钿乱颤；末了评定轻重，重者罚酒兼打手掌，轻者单罚酒，苏浣兰又替她们讨情。日暮席散，华夫人以"金菊对芙蓉"一令收煞，送客上轿而别。`,
    en: `Having seen his guest out, Xu Ziyun goes home and tells his wife Yuan Qixiang of the tenderness between Mei Ziyu and Qinyan. She asks what these performers have that her own maids lack, and he answers handsomely: they have a woman's face without a woman's body, so they delight the eye while governing the heart, giving pleasure without appetite — and besides, one cannot bring women to a banquet. She laughs at how well it sounds; he protests that he has never in his life said a thing he did not mean, and offers as proof that Mei Ziyu, teased that afternoon by Yuling, grew angry enough to abuse even his host. The next day is Lady Hua's twentieth birthday. Yuan Qixiang first sends ahead ten treasures — a linked jade ring, a kingfisher-feather fan, a coral hairpin, a jade-green qin, sandalwood zither pegs, a string of agarwood beads — and then follows in a perfumed carriage with six maids whose names all carry the character for red. Here the Hua household is set out in full: Hua Guangsu, twenty-one, commanding in style yet unspoiled in temper, a fine archer and a finer poet; his wife Su Huanxiang, daughter of the Minister of War, who with her sister Su Huanlan is known as the greater and lesser Su of the marquis's gate, so that the city says it would rather see them once than own ten pecks of dragon-palace pearls; ten beautiful maids of fifteen or sixteen whose names all carry the character for pearl, whose needlework outdoes heaven and whose talk is a series of small masterpieces; and a boys' troupe whose names all carry the character for years. Su Huanlan arrives first, Yuan Qixiang after, and Hua Guangsu, having paid respects at the family shrine, withdraws. The three women dine in the inner chamber, where Yuan Qixiang proposes a new game: throw six dice for a named pattern, then compose five rhyming lines — opening with a domino name, then a line of five-character Tang verse, then a phrase from the Western Chamber, then a tune title, then a line from the Mao Shi. Pattern after pattern comes up — crows clamouring in wind, an iron chain mooring a lone boat, the general hanging his seal, fallen petals filling the ground, two scholars entering Peach Blossom Spring, moonrise on a plum branch — and each woman answers in her own register, one voluptuous, one martial, one delicately chaste, toasting and praising and teasing by turns: Lady Hua suggests that Yuan Qixiang's verses complain of a husband who comes home drunk from the garden with actors, and Yuan Qixiang retorts that Lady Hua is plainly remembering her wedding nights. She also praises Wang Qionghua, daughter of the Commissioner of Transmission, who at seventeen dashed off Lantern and Moon Lyrics running to seven or eight hundred characters, strange and dazzling, as though Li Bai had come again. In the back room the ten pearls and the six reds play the same game, evenly matched in age and looks and equally determined to win, so that every verse composed on another's behalf carries a barb; they shriek, shove, and overturn a cup, until the ladies send a servant to investigate. Meaning to be severe, the two mistresses instead hear the six most insulting compositions recited back to them and laugh until their hairpins tremble; in the end the worst offenders are fined wine and struck on the palm, the milder ones fined only, and Su Huanlan begs them off. At dusk Lady Hua closes the game with a graceful verse on golden chrysanthemum matched with lotus, and the guests are handed into their sedan chairs.`,
  },
  12: {
    zh: `袁绮香归家将华府所行酒令一一说与徐子云，子云竟瞒着夫人把十六个令刻版印行，分作上下二等，因首令是"群鸦噪凤"，遂题为群鸦噪凤令，外间传为美谈；及夫人知道，已流布无从追回。仲春时节，书中正式引出一位要角：田春航，号湘帆，金陵人而寄居扬州，年二十三。其父田浩中进士即殁于京师，母田太夫人张氏苦节抚孤，学问多得母教，十三岁进学，十八岁中副举，貌朗如玉山，情性蕴藉，只是三试不第。去年奉母命游学京师，携老仆田安北上，先在杭州、苏州盘桓半年，诗文传抄殆遍，人谓可与侯太史、屈本立争名，因而囊橐充盈；又绕道东瞻泰岱、西谒华山，十一月底方抵京，寓城南宏济寺，恰与高品前后隔院。他嫌京中妓女粗俗，专心听戏，偏又不爱昆腔而爱乱弹，被几个下作相公迷住，做衣买器、随分子，数月间千金散尽，衣服典当将完，那些相公便渐渐疏远，他却毫不介意，只抱定"财尽而交绝"，能乐一日算一日。高品见他青蔬白饭而待人极厚，讥他"待己如此之薄，待人如此之厚"，春航便索性引《孟子》"知好色则慕少艾"一路痛驳：好女而不好男终是好淫，既重色自不敢淫；又说造物费尽气力生出这些人物，正要缙绅名士品题赏识，若吝己之财而爱人之色，是以烂臭粪土换奇香宝花。高品无从驳起，只说他床头金尽，恐为郑元和所笑。恰值颜仲清来访，高品讲起怡园两封灯谜情书被同庙的田湘帆猜破，仲清一听"湘帆"二字，便记起去年酒楼粉壁题词的少年，即请引见；相见之下果是其人，细问家世，才知春航之妻竟是他的本家叔父之女，于是亲上加亲，谈得十分投契。仲清劝他所赏识的玉美、翠宝、蓉官不过狐媚善妒，视钱财为性命，不如史南湘《曲台花选》中袁宝珠、苏蕙芳一流可作良友；春航听了默然，却仍说赏识须自具眼力，又发下宏愿：他日得志，当起金屋数重，将这班人尽配作玉瑟瑶琴，还普天下八万三千大心愿。仲清怜其才，先赠二百金试其心，不上半月又化为乌有。一日春航在联珠班看戏，衣衫敝旧坐于暗处，蓉官竟迟疑半晌不敢招呼，末了才过来请安；富伦、魏聘才在旁窃议"这种人有什么巴结处"，蓉官红着脸回护，说他从不欠人开发、不要人替担账。散场时细雨路滑，一辆骡车擦身而过，春航一滑跌了满身烂泥；车中人急令勒住牲口、扶起路人，探身连连道歉——那是个如冰雪抟成、明月梨花般的绝色相公，春航一腔怒气顿消，反以两只泥手扶着车沿连说不妨。回庙后他向高品描摹其容貌衣色，高品戏作一章"其雨其雨，梨园之东"的拟《诗经》体，猜是苏蕙芳，又戏言见了第一名只怕要跌进沟里。次日春航私取田安的茧绸皮袍去当了六吊钱，一连看了五天联锦班的戏，终于见着那人唱一出《独占》，访得果然是苏蕙芳；蕙芳认出这位泥中人，向他微微一笑，他便喜得说不出话，自此日思夜梦，几成相思。及至煤米全无、房钱催逼，他才想起写信向仲清求援。仲清却只遣人送来唐寅所画鹑衣乞食的郑元和小像，上题两首七绝相讥。春航大怒，当即挥笔和作，说"欲使蛾眉成义侠，忍教骏骨暂支离""五陵年少休相薄，后日功名若个如"。仲清与王恂看了，知其志不可夺，又闻他已断炊，便再备百金交高品相机点化。`,
    en: `Yuan Qixiang comes home and recites every forfeit from the Hua banquet to Xu Ziyun, who — without telling her — has all sixteen cut on blocks and printed, ranked in two classes, and titled after the first pattern, Crows Clamouring Round the Phoenix. The set is admired all over town, and by the time his wife learns of it there is no recalling them. Mid-spring brings forward one of the novel's central figures: Tian Chunhang, styled Xiangfan, of Jinling but settled in Yangzhou, twenty-three years old. His father Tian Hao died in the capital in the very year he took his jinshi; his mother, Lady Tian, kept her widowhood and taught the boy herself, so that he entered the district school at thirteen and took a supplementary degree at eighteen. He is bright as a jade mountain and gracious in temper, but has failed three provincial examinations. Sent north last year to study, with the old servant Tian An in attendance, he lingered half a year in Hangzhou and Suzhou, where his writings were copied everywhere and men said he could rival Hou Taishi and Qu Benli, and where his purse filled accordingly; then he came by way of Mount Tai and Mount Hua, reaching Beijing only at the end of the eleventh month and lodging at the Hongji Temple across the courtyard from Gao Pin. Finding the capital's courtesans coarse, he gave himself to the theatre — and, preferring the rough regional style to kunqu, fell in with several worthless performers, buying them clothes and trinkets until a thousand taels were gone and his own wardrobe pawned. As his money failed, the boys drifted off; he minded not at all, holding that when the money ends the friendship ends, and that a day's pleasure is a day gained. Gao Pin, finding him at a meal of plain rice and greens while lavishing everything on others, mocks him for treating himself so meanly and others so well — and Chunhang answers with a whole philosophy, citing Mencius on how a man who knows beauty longs for the young and fair: to love women and not men is mere lechery, whereas one who truly reveres beauty will not defile it; heaven took great trouble to make such creatures precisely so that gentlemen might appraise and cherish them; and to hoard one's own money while coveting another's beauty is to trade rotting dung for a fragrant jewel. Gao Pin cannot answer, and falls back on warning that an empty purse will make him a laughing-stock like Zheng Yuanhe. Just then Yan Zhongqing calls, and Gao Pin mentions that the two riddle love-letters at the Yi Garden were solved by a fellow lodger named Tian Xiangfan — at which Zhongqing remembers the youth who inscribed the tavern wall the previous winter and asks to be introduced. It proves to be the same man, and on questioning it emerges that Chunhang's wife is the daughter of Zhongqing's own uncle, so that friendship is doubled by kinship. Zhongqing argues that the performers Chunhang favours — Yumei, Cuibao, Rongguan — are merely coquettes who treat money as life and clothing as affection, and that the names in Shi Nanxiang's Qutai Huaxuan, Yuan Baozhu and Su Huifang above all, are fit to be friends. Chunhang falls silent, but maintains that discernment must be one's own, and declares that if fortune ever favours him he will raise golden houses and settle the whole company in them, fulfilling eighty-three thousand wishes at a stroke. Pitying his gifts, Zhongqing sends two hundred taels to test him; within a fortnight they have evaporated. One day at the Lianzhu theatre, seated in shadow in worn clothes, Chunhang finds that Rongguan hesitates a long while before daring to greet him, while Fu Lun and Wei Pincai remark audibly that such a man can hardly be worth cultivating — and Rongguan, colouring, defends him as one who never left a bill unpaid or asked another to stand surety. Leaving in the drizzle, he is clipped by a mule carriage and goes down full length in the mud. The passenger cries out to hold the beast and lift the man, leaning from the window to apologise again and again: a performer of surpassing beauty, moulded of snow and jade, like pear blossom under moonlight. Chunhang's anger vanishes; he steadies himself on the carriage rail with two muddy hands and insists it was his own carelessness. Back at the temple he describes the face and clothes to Gao Pin, who composes a mock ode in the manner of The Classic of Poetry on rain east of the theatre, guesses Su Huifang, and jokes that the sight of the register's first name would land him in a ditch. Next morning Chunhang quietly pawns Tian An's silk-lined fur robe for six strings and watches the Lianjin troupe five days running until at last the same performer appears in Exclusive Possession, and enquiry confirms it is Su Huifang. Huifang, recognising the man from the mud, gives him the faintest smile, and he is left speechless with joy, then sleepless with longing. Only when the coal and rice run out and the monks press for rent does he write to Zhongqing for help — and receives in reply Tang Yin's painting of Zheng Yuanhe begging in rags, inscribed with two cutting quatrains. Furious, Chunhang answers on the spot in matching verse: to make a beauty into a woman of honour, one must let a fine horse go lean awhile; let the young lords of the capital not despise him, for whose future will match his? Reading it, Zhongqing and Wang Xun recognise a will that cannot be turned, and hearing that his fire is now cold, they put together another hundred taels and leave it with Gao Pin to use as occasion serves.`,
  },
  13: {
    zh: `颜仲清将王恂所备百金送到高品处充作薪水，春航告贷无门，只得暂时寄食。他一心记着苏蕙芳，无钱听戏，便天天站在联锦班戏园门口守候，看他上车下车，如醉如痴，一连十余日。蕙芳早认出这是那日跌在泥中的人，见他面带憔悴而珊珊玉骨、情韵盎然，心生怜念，屡以秋波相赠；一日索性命跟班的请他进门。春航羞涩不敢入，经再三相请才整衣而进，但见满院嫣红姹紫，小小客厅钟鼎琴书，十分精雅。蕙芳出来见礼，二人并立几乎一般高；春航见壁上悬着度香、静宜、竹君、剑潭、前舟、庸庵六人和韵的七律，方知他竟能作诗，更加起敬。问起出身，蕙芳眼圈一红，细述前情：本是官宦人家，随父宦游云南，八岁丧母，十二岁上父亲被参劾气病而亡，两袖清风，仅存的箱笼又被亲戚长随瓜分，只剩一个老家人相依，辗转一年零两月回到苏州，亲邻冷眼，一饭难求；再搭粮船北上投亲，那亲戚却已发配口外。走到前门，幸遇蒙师顾先生——此人也曾把银钱听戏听尽，索性烧了文章入了联锦班唱小生——收留他读书写字、讲究诗词；不料老家人进京两月即病故，先生也于去年夏天染疫而亡，他这才孤身立于梨园。春航听得也伤心，遂发一番议论：人生有四等好友，一曰好天，二曰好地，三曰好书，四曰好诗，然天不能常好、地不能尽游、书多灾梨祸枣、诗每再吟如嚼蜡，故皆可宝而非至宝；惟有相公一流，色、体、容、态、情、妆、艺俱全，才是四友之外的"宝友"。蕙芳听得又是感动又觉太过，心中已把那些富贵王孙尽压下去。二人对酌吐露肺腑，蕙芳临别执其手立誓："今日订交，此生勿负"，又嘱他从此不必再来戏园守候，须自重努力前程，自己每日必去看他一次。次日晨起，张仲雨竟同着开起盛银号的潘其观登门。这潘其观山西籍而两代居京，坐拥百万，开着三个银号两个当铺，为人鄙吝龌龊而好色，偏又惧内到极处，续娶之妻一见他夜不归宿便领人搜遍窑子，因此他动了买娈童的念头，看中蕙芳，重金托张仲雨说合。席间他絮絮肉麻，竟拉住蕙芳的手要他做干儿子，说"我又没有儿子，要这分家财作什么"。蕙芳几乎气哭，转念心生一计，假意殷勤劝酒，又借口欠人银两，向他张口借二百两；潘其观凑了二百吊钱票递上，蕙芳收进靴掖，便含酒作势敬了个"皮杯"，随即假称鲠住喉咙，只说潘三多饮几杯他便不疼——两人被灌到十二分，先后翻倒在地。蕙芳吩咐把二人扶到客厅炕上同盖一被，笑说"想吃天鹅肉，自作自受"，随即收拾五套衣服连那二百吊钱票，带着铺盖乘车去寻春航。到胡同口，月下正见春航痴立——他候了一日不见人来，已在门外徘徊多时。到了庙中，蕙芳见他行李萧条，将衣服钱票尽数留下，说"你不受，便看轻我了"，二人直谈到鸡鸣，成了道义之交。那边潘其观四更酒醒，摸黑撒了一鞋的尿，又把张仲雨错认成蕙芳一把搂住乱扯；仲雨被摇醒，一口秽物尽吐在他脸上，两人互骂"兔子""你爹"，扭打滚倒，惊动满家掌灯来看，只见一个光着脚坐在地下满面狼藉，一个踏着棉被叉手而立，笑倒众人。等到天明认清了彼此，才拍手大笑。问起蕙芳，家人只说华公子昨夜遣人来接，已进城去了，二人无可奈何，白丢了二百吊钱，郁郁而去。`,
    en: `Yan Zhongqing leaves Wang Xun's hundred taels with Gao Pin as a maintenance fund, and Chunhang, with nowhere else to turn, accepts board from his neighbour. His mind is wholly on Su Huifang; lacking the price of a seat, he simply stands at the door of the Lianjin theatre day after day to watch her come and go, rapt and unblinking, for more than ten days together. Huifang has recognised the man from the mud, and seeing him worn but finely made, she begins to answer his gaze; at last she has a servant call him in. He is too abashed to enter until asked three times, then straightens his clothes and follows into a courtyard blazing with late-spring flowers and a small hall of bronzes, books, and a qin. When she comes out to greet him they stand almost of a height. On her wall hang six regulated poems written in matching rhymes by Duxiang, Jingyi, Zhujun, Jiantan, Qianzhou, and Yong'an, and Chunhang, realising she composes verse herself, grows more respectful still. Asked about her origins, her eyes redden and the story comes out: she was born to an official family and travelled with her father to Yunnan; her mother died when she was eight; at twelve her father was impeached, sickened with rage, and died within the month, leaving nothing, and what little remained was carved up by relatives and retainers, so that only one old servant stayed with her. It took them a year and two months to reach Suzhou, where neighbours looked coldly on and not a meal could be had; they took a grain boat north to a kinsman in Zhili, only to learn he had been banished beyond the frontier. At the city gate she happened on her old tutor, Master Gu — a man who had likewise spent his money on the theatre and ended by burning his essays and joining the Lianjin troupe as a young-male lead — who took them in and taught her reading and verse. But the old servant died of the journey within two months, and the tutor of an epidemic last summer, leaving her alone in the theatre. Moved, Chunhang offers a discourse of his own: a man may have four excellent friends — fine weather, fine country, fine books, fine poems — yet the weather turns, the country cannot all be walked, most books are waste paper, and a poem admired at night tastes of wax by morning; all are precious but none supreme. Only performers, complete in colour, form, face, bearing, feeling, adornment, and art, are the treasure-friend beyond those four. Huifang finds it moving and excessive at once, but from that hour every rich young lord she knows has been eclipsed. They drink together and speak from the heart, and at parting she takes his hand and swears that the friendship sealed this day will not be betrayed, forbidding him to wait outside the theatre again, urging him to look to his own future, and promising to visit him daily. The next morning Zhang Zhongyu arrives with Pan Qiguan of the Qisheng bank — a Shanxi man of the second generation in the capital, worth a million, master of three banks and two pawnshops, mean, filthy, and lecherous, yet so terrified of his wife that a single night away brings her out in a carriage to search the brothels for him. Hence his scheme to buy a boy; he has settled on Huifang and has paid Zhongyu to negotiate. Over the wine he grows unbearable, seizing Huifang's hand and proposing that the boy become his adopted son, since he has no heir and asks what all his money is for. Huifang, nearly weeping with rage, changes tack: she plays the willing host, then pleads a debt and asks to borrow two hundred taels. Pan Qiguan produces two hundred strings in notes, which she tucks into her boot before offering him a mouth-to-mouth cup, then feigns a bone caught in her throat that only eases, she says, when Pan drinks another. Both men are poured to the brim and go over backwards, and Huifang has them laid on the hall couch under a single quilt, remarking that those who covet swan's flesh may enjoy what they get. She then packs five suits of her own clothes and the notes, takes bedding, and drives to find Chunhang — meeting him at the lane's mouth in the moonlight, where he has been loitering since evening, having waited all day for her. Seeing how bare his lodging is, she leaves everything, telling him that refusing would be to think little of her, and they talk until cockcrow, their bond now one of principle rather than desire. Back at her house Pan Qiguan wakes at the fourth watch, urinates into his own shoe in the dark, then gropes his way onto the couch, mistakes Zhang Zhongyu for Huifang, and embraces him; Zhongyu, shaken awake, is sick over his face. The two abuse each other as rabbit and father, grapple, and go down, rousing the household, who arrive with lamps to find one man seated on the floor, barefoot and befouled, pointing at the other, who stands on the trampled quilt. By daylight they recognise each other and burst out laughing. Told that Young Master Hua sent for Huifang in the night and she has gone into the city, they can do nothing — two hundred strings lost — and depart in silence.`,
  },
  14: {
    zh: `蕙芳亲手替春航解衣换上新制的小毛衣裳、新靴新帽，又拿镜子倚着他一照，映出两个玉人。春航心上一荡，忽念此人是患难知己，岂可稍涉邪念，便敛神自持。蕙芳翻检书案，得他的诗稿《燕台旅稿》，随手一揭正是一首《恼公》七古，遂低声吟诵，句句品评：说这一路是学温、李三十六体，纤秾之极；赞"狸奴"句胜似"燕子"，"飘烟抱月一尺腰，星眸欲妒春云娇"更妙不可言。及至"日近云鬟身不动"，便笑问既已如愿，何必天天上戏园；又猜诗中人非袁瑶卿即金瘦香，春航只摇头说是"寓言空空"之人。念到"井底水如妾心意，路旁尘惹君身衣"，蕙芳凄然作色，叹一句一往情深。高品进来，偏说这一句该改作"车前泥染君身衣"才真切有味，又故意占了一卦，念出"田获一兔，往遇雨，需于泥"的爻辞来打趣，说自己详不出来。庙里的唐和尚也钻进来凑趣，合掌说佛殿上龙女菩萨不见了，原来在此；高品揪下他的帽子出对"若锥处囊中，颖脱而出"，蕙芳应声对以"如飘浮水面，顶圆而光"，敏捷工巧，满座称好。饭后蕙芳要听琴，春航和了弦，先弹《结客少年场》三段，抑扬顿挫，索铃短锁诸般指法迭出；蕙芳又要他弹《胡笳十八拍》《洞天春晓》，说这两套曾听萧次贤弹得最好，静宜还教过他吹箫，春航却择了一套十二段的《水仙操》，弹到中段如湘娥啼竹、列子御风，如怨如慕，高品与蕙芳都正襟危坐。忽然弦音自乱，春航停手说：必有会弹琴的人在外听着——院中果然爆出一阵狂笑，原来是史南湘挟着王兰保、携着李玉林，带着几分酒意闯来，手中还拈着怡园折来的杏花。南湘久闻田湘帆之名，当即请见，一见如故，谈琴谱文艺，彼此敬服。高品戏称"史竹君是梨园的狄梁公，田湘帆是戏班的李药师"，又把春航跌进车辙的旧事一五一十抖了出来，羞得他无地自容；南湘则笑赠一联"孙寿堕妆，梁冀下马；苏郎唱戏，田子跟车"。蕙芳反唇，把刘文泽替高品编的一长串"曲部尚书、世袭一等史国公"的滑稽官衔一口气背出，笑倒一堂。席间行"奚"字令，从《四书》中依次找出第一至第十一个奚字，抢说、错说、说两句的层出不穷，罚酒动辄五杯九杯十八杯，兰保、玉林、蕙芳纷纷代饮。中途蕙芳家中来人回报：昨夜那两个醉汉已各自回去，此刻另有一位广东来的奚十一老爷慕名候着，带着四个跟班，光景很阔。蕙芳只回说今日不归——这位新客的名字，就此第一次落到他门上。二更后席散，蕙芳先归，南湘送兰保、玉林回家，路过王恂门口口渴，便进去寻王恂、颜仲清说话。`,
    en: `Huifang unfastens Chunhang's coat with her own hands, dresses him in newly made furs, new boots and cap, then leans beside him with a mirror so that two jade faces show in it together. Something turns over in him; he reminds himself sharply that she is the friend of his adversity and no object of appetite, and steadies himself. Turning over his desk she finds his manuscript, Travel Drafts from Yantai, opens it at a long poem in the old style titled Annoying the Lord, and reads it aloud with running commentary: this manner descends from Wen Tingyun and Li Shangyin at their most sumptuous; the line on the cat outdoes the line on the swallows; the couplet on a waist that holds the moon and eyes that shame the spring clouds is finer still. Coming to a line about being near the beloved's hair and not stirring, she asks with a smile why, being so satisfied, he still haunted the theatre daily, and guesses the subject must be Yuan Yaoqing or Jin Shouxiang; Chunhang only shakes his head and calls the figure an invention. At the couplet comparing her heart to water at the bottom of a well and his robe to dust from the roadside, she turns grave and murmurs that here is a man of deep feeling. Gao Pin comes in to say the line would gain by reading \"mud before the carriage stains your robe,\" and then pretends to cast a hexagram, reciting an oracle about catching a hare, meeting rain, and waiting in the mud, which he claims he cannot interpret. Monk Tang from the temple squeezes in too, palms together, announcing that the dragon-maiden bodhisattva has vanished from the hall and is evidently here; Gao Pin snatches his cap and offers a couplet about an awl in a bag whose point works through, to which Huifang instantly answers with one about a gourd afloat on water, round-topped and shining — quick and exact, and much admired. After the meal she asks for music. Chunhang tunes and plays three sections of Young Gallants Gathering Clients, full of ringing bell-strokes and short locks, then, when she asks for Eighteen Songs of a Nomad Flute or Spring Dawn in the Grotto Heaven — pieces she has heard Xiao Cixian play best, for Jingyi once taught her the flute — chooses instead the twelve sections of the Narcissus Lyric. By the middle sections it is like the goddess weeping on the bamboo, like Liezi riding the wind, plaintive and reproachful, and both listeners sit upright and still. Then the tuning slips of itself, and Chunhang stops: someone outside must know the qin. A burst of laughter from the courtyard proves him right — Shi Nanxiang has arrived half drunk with Wang Lanbao on one arm and Li Yulin on the other, carrying apricot blossom picked at the Yi Garden. Having heard of Tian Xiangfan, he asks to be introduced, and the two fall at once into talk of scores and letters, each impressed. Gao Pin dubs Shi Zhujun the theatre's great minister of talent and Tian Xiangfan its great strategist, then retails the whole story of the fall in the mud until Chunhang has nowhere to hide, while Nanxiang composes a mocking couplet pairing famous lovers of antiquity with an actor on stage and a scholar trotting after a carriage. Huifang retaliates by reciting, at a breath, the absurd chain of offices Liu Wenze once invented for Gao Pin, ending in Minister of the Music Bureau and Duke of the Shi State, first class, hereditary. A drinking game follows on the character \"Xi,\" each player required to find its first through eleventh occurrences in the Four Books; men speak out of turn, cite two lines at once, and are fined five, nine, even eighteen cups, with Lanbao, Yulin, and Huifang drinking on others' behalf. Midway a messenger comes from Huifang's house: the two drunkards have gone home, but a Cantonese gentleman named Xi Shiyi is waiting there, richly attended by four servants, having come to pay his respects to her fame. She sends word that she will not be back today — and so this new caller's name arrives at her door for the first time. The party breaks up after the second watch; Huifang leaves first, and Nanxiang, seeing Lanbao and Yulin home, stops at Wang Xun's gate for a drink of water and goes in to sit with Wang Xun and Yan Zhongqing.`,
  },
  15: {
    zh: `史南湘回到王恂、颜仲清处，将田春航与苏蕙芳患难订交、诗酒相怜的情景细细说来，二人艳羡之余，也知春航从此或能收心。梅宅这边，西席李性全已选安徽知县，魏聘才替他张罗盘缠，李元茂则打算寄籍京中。聘才趁忌辰独往樱桃巷，会叶茂林、打茶围，竟在杜琴言处说动一番相思，约定次日带梅子玉相见；子玉一夜不眠，正待赴约，却传来父亲梅士燮放江西学差的喜讯，府中贺客盈门，相会之期遂被官场大事隔断。临行前，颜夫人提醒士燮将二女儿与王家的婚约补足礼数，孙亮功作媒，王文辉回礼丰厚；席间孙亮功又看中衣冠焕然的李元茂，经仲清推波助澜，为他与孙家女另伏一桩婚姻。子玉闷坐无趣，往访刘文泽、史南湘，反被二人取笑与琴言"定情"，强拉去樱桃巷。琴言偏被刘大人带往春喜园，众人转入对门妙香堂拜陆素兰。素兰一见便猜出子玉姓名，又说琴言自正月初七后日日来访，只因自己与子玉相似，常错唤他作"庾香"，借他作一幅小影以慰相思；子玉听得酸泪暗吞，受素兰赠湘妃竹纸扇而归。到三月初十，梅士燮出京赴任，亲友在旗亭饯别，子玉等送至前站宿店，次日洒泪叩别。徐子云送行后回怡园桃花坞，与萧次贤、袁宝珠、金漱芳赏花行令；宝珠、漱芳仿袁绮香所刻酒令掷骰作句，或绿暗红稀，或打破锦屏风，妙语迭出。花影酒痕之间，众人又谈起子玉、琴言与春航、蕙芳两段深情，次贤预言这相得之后，未来必有许多苦恼缓缓生出。`,
    en: `Shi Nanxiang returns to Wang Xun and Yan Zhongqing and recounts, with relish, the new intimacy between Tian Chunhang and Su Huifang, leaving both listeners envious and half-convinced that Chunhang may finally reform. In the Mei household, tutor Li Xingquan has been appointed county magistrate in Anhui; Wei Pincai helps raise his travel funds, while Li Yuanmao schemes to register himself in the capital. Pincai meanwhile visits Cherry Blossom Lane, drinks with Ye Maolin, makes the rounds of the actors' tea tables, and at last reaches Du Qinyan, where he embroiders Ziyu's longing so persuasively that Qinyan agrees to meet him the next day. Ziyu lies awake all night, only for the appointment to be swept aside by great family news: Mei Shixie has been named Academic Commissioner of Jiangxi, and the house fills with congratulatory callers. Before departing, Madam Yan reminds Shixie to regularize his second daughter's engagement to the Wang family, with Sun Lianggong as go-between and Wang Wenhui sending handsome return gifts; in the same festive current, Lianggong notices the newly dressed Yuanmao, and Zhongqing quietly helps set in motion a match between Yuanmao and the Sun daughter. Restless, Ziyu visits Liu Wenze and Shi Nanxiang, who tease him mercilessly about having "pledged affection" with Qinyan and drag him to Cherry Blossom Lane. Qinyan is away at Chunxi Garden, so they cross to Lu Sulan's Miaoxiang Hall. Sulan guesses Ziyu's name at sight, then reveals the wound at the chapter's heart: since the seventh day of the first month Qinyan has visited him daily because Sulan resembles Ziyu, even slipping and calling him "Yuxiang," borrowing his face as a painted miniature of the beloved. Ziyu swallows tears, accepts a freshly written fan from Sulan, and leaves. On the tenth day of the third month, Shixie departs; friends feast him at a roadside inn, and Ziyu escorts him to the first station before weeping home. Xu Ziyun, returning from the farewell, enters the peach grove at Yiyuan, where Xiao Cixian, Yuan Baozhu, and Jin Shufang drink among blossoms and compose new dice-game forfeits in the style of Yuan Qixiang's printed set. Amid wine, peach light, and clever lines, the company speaks of the two great attachments now unfolding — Ziyu and Qinyan, Chunhang and Huifang — and Cixian quietly predicts that such perfect sympathy will not come without future suffering.`,
  },
  16: {
    zh: `梅士燮赴任之后，内有颜夫人掌家，外有许顺经理，井井有条。子玉仍旧读书，经籍之外兼研诸子百家，花晨月夕则与刘文泽、颜仲清辈明窗净几，把酒清谈、题诗分韵；徐子云一月也过访几次，只是他局面阔大，干谒纷纷，反不及子玉这般清闲自在。魏聘才却在梅宅一无所事：梅士燮远宦江西要两三年才回，王文辉又大模大样毫无关切，他自忖"长安虽好，非久恋之乡"，不如另觅一个居停主人攀鳞附翼，遂进城去找富伦。富伦在对门贵芬家中打牌，聘才顺势拜了贵大爷，席上又认识两人：一个是华府的老篾片阎简安，与老公爷三十年交情而心窄嘴臭；一个是号梅窗、行八的杨梅窗，富伦的干兄弟，借看风水为名而实不通堪舆，专一贪财勾通。杨梅窗与聘才一拍即合，当场便要换帖；阎简安却势利透顶，两下相厌。散后聘才把心事说与富伦，富伦便荐他到"舅太爷"华公子府中帮办杂务，说那里只须陪酒陪戏、写些不要紧的书札，会弹唱更合心意，只要多戴高帽便可。次日富伦果然去说妥，华公子一口应允，吩咐林珊枝传谕总办议定束修伙食。聘才向子玉辞行，子玉吃惊挽留，李元茂也劝他别去吃钉板，他只说各人有各人的打算；颜夫人念其能办事，仍送了四十两银子饯行。次早两辆车来接，富伦亲送入府。但见照墙三丈、石狮八尺，头门到二门一箭之遥，甬道百步方至大厅，长廊迭阁、碧瓦琉璃，每一重门各有总管登记出入。西花厅中鸭绿绒毯、王铎题匾、王维山水、文徵明草书，锦天绣地，看得聘才心生畏惧。因公子身上不爽快，便安置他到东花园与画工笔人物的张笑梅、画山水花草的顾月卿同住，另有书启先生王卿云。是晚公子送来一桌四十余样的酒席，众人直饮到二更；次日又一一见过总管、账房、司阍、司厨、库房各色人等。侯门如海，聘才初来只敢小心恭维——自此他进了华府，日后多少事端便由此生出。这边子玉思念了几日，四月中旬芍药盛开，他忽忆起陆素兰待他的情分，检出古砚、香墨、墨兰册页与团扇，又用小楷写了送春二律，亲自送去。素兰谢过，却叹一口气道破琴言的心事：自怡园同席以后，他便不大招呼人，说话总要扯到梅花上去，请萧次贤画了四幅梅花，又向徐子云讨南边织来的折枝梅绸缎做了袍袄；连师傅都说他近来无缘无故酸酸楚楚。子玉听得泪下，半晌才把途中所闻、会馆看《惊梦》、怡园为假琴官所戏、屏后呜咽诸事一一说出，末了却问：我为他是知他底蕴，他为我又晓得我什么？素兰亦为之凄然，说度香有言"人各有缘，此中系天定"，又叹《国风》好色而不淫，正是庾香、玉侬之谓。二人同往秋水堂，门前歇着五六辆车，只得退回打听：琴言卧病在床，师傅长庆正请部里几位经承吃分子饭；再探时前客虽散，又来一班不相识的人硬要叫琴言，长庆推说不在家，他们竟闯进房去抢了烟枪，扬言要到兵马司出首，闹得满堂是人。子玉长叹一声，托素兰致意琴言保重，又说度香处须常去走动，约定改日到素兰这里相会。归途上迎面撞见王通政王文辉，慌得要下车，只勉强应对几句——这一日访友不遇，反遇见了丈人，愈觉纳闷。`,
    en: `With Mei Shixie gone to his post, Lady Yan manages the household within and Xu Shun without, and all runs smoothly. Ziyu keeps to his books, ranging beyond the classics into the philosophers, and on fine mornings and moonlit evenings sits with Liu Wenze and Yan Zhongqing over wine and rhymes; Xu Ziyun calls a few times a month, though his own establishment is so grand and his callers so many that he enjoys less leisure than these quieter friends. Wei Pincai, meanwhile, has nothing to do. Mei Shixie will be two or three years in Jiangxi, Wang Wenhui is grand and indifferent, and Pincai concludes that fine as the capital is, it is no place to linger without a patron. He drives into the city to find Fu Lun, who is at cards across the road at Gui Fen's, and so pays his respects to Eldest Master Gui as well. Two other players are there: Yan Jian'an, an old hanger-on of the Hua household, thirty years a friend of the old duke but narrow and foul-tongued; and Yang Meichuang, eighth of his line, Fu Lun's sworn brother, who trades on a reputation as a geomancer while understanding nothing of the art and everything of profit. Yang Meichuang takes to Pincai at once and proposes they exchange cards of brotherhood; Yan Jian'an, snobbish to the bone, despises him, and the feeling is mutual. Afterwards Pincai opens his mind to Fu Lun, who offers to recommend him to his wife's uncle, Young Master Hua — the post requires only attendance at wine and theatre and the writing of inconsequential letters, and since Pincai can sing it will suit all the better; flattery is the whole secret. Fu Lun makes the arrangement next day, Hua agrees readily, and Lin Shanzhi is told to settle the salary with the steward. Pincai takes his leave of Ziyu, who is startled and presses him to stay, while Li Yuanmao warns that he is walking onto a bed of nails; Pincai answers that every man must reckon for himself. Lady Yan, valuing his usefulness, sends him off with forty taels. Two carriages come at dawn, and Fu Lun escorts him in person. The screen wall is thirty feet high, the stone lions eight; it is a bowshot from the outer gate to the second and a hundred paces more to the great hall, through galleries and tiered pavilions under glazed tiles, with a steward at every gate to record who passes. In the west flower hall — duck-green carpet, a plaque by Wang Duo, landscapes by Wang Wei, cursive script by Wen Zhengming — the sheer magnificence makes Pincai afraid. The young master being unwell, he is lodged instead in the east garden with Zhang Xiaomei, who paints figures in fine line, and Gu Yueqing, who paints landscape and flowers, along with the secretary Wang Qingyun. That evening a banquet of more than forty dishes arrives from the master, and the company drinks to the second watch; next day Pincai is presented to the stewards, the counting house, the gatekeepers, the kitchen, and the storerooms. The gate of a noble house is deep as the sea, and for now he keeps small and flatters everyone — but from this day Wei Pincai is inside the Hua mansion, and much will come of it. Ziyu, missing him for some days, finds the peonies at their height in mid-fourth month and remembers Lu Sulan's kindness; he selects an old inkstone, two boxes of scented ink, an album of ink orchids, and a round fan, copies out two farewell-to-spring poems in small script, and takes them himself. Sulan thanks him, then sighs and lays bare Qinyan's condition: since the evening at the Yi Garden he has hardly spoken to anyone, steers every conversation round to plum blossom, has had Xiao Cixian paint him four plum studies, and begged from Xu Ziyun bolts of southern silk figured with plum sprays to be made into gowns; even his teacher says he has turned inexplicably melancholy. Ziyu weeps, and at last tells the whole story — what Pincai reported on the road, the performance of Waking from a Dream at the guild hall, the trick played on him with the false Qinguan, the sound of stifled weeping behind the screen — and ends by asking what the boy can possibly know of him, when he at least knows the boy's history. Sulan, moved to tears herself, quotes Duxiang's saying that each has his own destined affinity, and observes that the Airs of the States, praising desire without licence, fits Yuxiang and Yunong exactly. They walk to the Autumn Water Hall, but five or six carriages stand at the door and they must turn back and send to enquire: Qinyan is ill in bed while his teacher Changqing entertains clerks from the ministry. A second enquiry brings worse news — those guests gone, a party of strangers has arrived demanding Qinyan, refused to leave when told he is out, forced their way into Changqing's room, seized his opium pipe, and threatened to denounce him to the police, so that the house is full of idlers and uproar. Ziyu sighs, asks Sulan to urge Qinyan to take care of himself and to keep calling on Duxiang, and arranges to meet him here another day. Driving home he runs headlong into Wang Wenhui's carriage and must scramble to get down and pay his respects — a day of friends not found and a father-in-law encountered instead.`,
  },
  17: {
    zh: `子玉自素兰处归来，月明花静，正自怅望，高品、史南湘、颜仲清、王恂联袂而至。众人先说日间在高品斋中会了田春航与苏蕙芳，赞湘帆果是同道，与媚香相处的光景令人艳羡；高品便借"惧内"大作文章，说湘帆守定约法三章，连王恂、史南湘辈也牢守闺房，不奉将令不敢妄离一步，满座哄然。继而高品提出：十五日是蕙芳生日，不如凑一公分，在刘文泽的倚剑眠琴之室设席，为春航、蕙芳同贺。点名之间，王恂先漏了徐子云、萧次贤，众人补上；南湘又故意提醒"大会群花，倒忘了一个花王"，这才添上杜琴言，并及秦琪官，共成十人。子玉听见琴言名字，心头一震，散后独坐磨墨，写给度香、静宜的请帖竟成一篇骈俪小启，"怜渠蕙质，堕彼梨园；会我竹林，数他花信"，字字香艳。次日文泽在家布置，并知会诸花辰刻毕集，只是琪官害眼、琴言病未痊愈，未必能来；席间酒令则议来议去，只说到时见景生情。此时的春航已足不出户，蕙芳每日必来一次，或清谈或小饮，时进箴砭；他一意研经挥翰，名动京师而耻于干谒，宁居萧寺；蕙芳又替他结交了度香、静宜、文泽、南湘、仲清、王恂诸人，仲清昔日相激原是激励，经高品剖明，又添赠五十金，两下更觉相好。及至开筵，中堂高悬群仙高会之图，长廊摆列十余盆蕙花。蕙芳华服先至，恭行大礼，说"今日大罗天上，众仙齐集，使蕙芳辈鸡犬偕升"；子玉与他初次觌面，彼此暗暗赞叹，蕙芳心中直以瑜亮并生相许。徐子云、萧次贤携袁宝珠、王兰保并八个书童翩然而来。高品又打趣春航迟到，说他莫非又滑倒在车辙里，惹得满座大笑，蕙芳脸红。众人不肯推寿翁寿母上坐，仍旧叙齿分作三席，由金漱芳、李玉林、林春喜、袁宝珠、王兰保、陆素兰、苏蕙芳、王桂保分头伺候。酒酣之际，人人心畅，独子玉念着琴言卧病，药炉半烬，深闭绿窗，只推醉倚炕而卧；萧次贤说昨日诊脉，是积劳兼忧郁，痰中已有血点，非静养数月不能痊愈，子玉听得更觉烦闷。仲清提议留下翰墨，次贤应允作图，众人遂以年齿为序联句，从"玉树歌清晓莺乱"起，一路转韵，南湘一句"惟觉花心尚少真，蛱蝶有雄谁细辨"分清了人与花的界限，文泽以"鸳鸯虽小总相亲"工稳相对；高品屡出恶谑被群旦围啐，王恂一句"蝉娟疑窃嫦娥药"也遭他抢白；子玉心不在焉，只写下"顾盼曾回玉女眸"，暗指那一出《惊梦》，众人却不解其故；末了仍由子玉以"雪花飞上琼枝艳"作结，一气呵成。诗成又将人比花：宝珠为牡丹，蕙芳为芍药，素兰为莲花，玉林为碧桃，漱芳为海棠，兰保为玫瑰，桂保为芙蓉，春喜为兰花；子云说琴言色艺过佳而性情过冷，又酷爱梅，比作梅花最称，且不属庾香更属谁，说得子玉脸红；琪官则议定比作杏花。因说到菊有黄白，文泽提起东园新来一个从广西随千总入京、又被弃置的妓女，人称白菊花，高品便把她的来历一一说破。临散又订下十八日袁宝珠移寓之约，南湘夸口一客不到便罚他做一天东。是日之后，蕙芳踵门叩谢，独子玉已病。到十八日群贤毕集，自午至子，行令猜枚，清歌檀板；张仲雨也来趋奉，花谱中添了琪官，惟子玉与琴言二人皆未痊愈，南湘果然被罚，又在自己家中再叙一日。`,
    en: `Ziyu comes back from Sulan's into a night of moonlight and motionless flowers, and is still gazing out when Gao Pin, Shi Nanxiang, Yan Zhongqing, and Wang Xun arrive together. They begin with laughter: that day at Gao Pin's rooms they met Tian Chunhang and Su Huifang, and found Xiangfan truly one of their own, his life with Meixiang a thing to envy. Gao Pin turns it into a discourse on henpecked husbands — Xiangfan bound by a three-article covenant, Wang Xun and Shi Nanxiang never stirring from their quarters without orders — and the room dissolves. Then the real proposal: the fifteenth is Huifang's birthday, so let them pool a subscription and hold the feast at Liu Wenze's Chamber for Leaning on a Sword and Sleeping by the Qin, honouring Chunhang and Huifang together. Calling the roll, Wang Xun forgets Xu Ziyun and Xiao Cixian, who are duly added; then Nanxiang observes that a great assembly of flowers has somehow omitted its flower king, and Du Qinyan is added too, with Qin Qiguan, making ten. At Qinyan's name something turns over in Ziyu, and after the others go he sits alone grinding ink until his invitation to Duxiang and Jingyi has become a small parallel-prose rhapsody — pity for an orchid nature fallen into the theatre, a summons to a bamboo-grove fellowship, a counting of the flower-calendar winds. Next day Wenze prepares the rooms and the performers are told to gather at dawn; but Qiguan has an eye ailment and Qinyan is not recovered, so neither is certain, and the question of drinking games is left to the moment. Chunhang by now never leaves his rooms: Huifang comes once daily to talk or drink and to offer correction, while he grinds away at the classics, famous in the capital yet ashamed to solicit patrons, content to lodge in a temple. Through Huifang he has come to know Duxiang, Jingyi, Wenze, Nanxiang, Zhongqing, and Wang Xun; and since Gao Pin explained that Zhongqing's earlier scorn had been meant to goad him, and another fifty taels arrived, the two are warmer than ever. At the feast a painting of the immortals' assembly hangs in the hall and a dozen pots of orchid stand along the gallery. Huifang arrives first in full dress and makes a formal obeisance, saying that today the great heaven is crowded with immortals and even the dogs and fowl of his sort may rise with them. He and Ziyu meet for the first time, each privately marvelling, Huifang deciding that here is a rival talent worthy of his own Tian. Xu Ziyun and Xiao Cixian sweep in with Yuan Baozhu, Wang Lanbao, and eight page boys. Gao Pin teases the late Chunhang by wondering whether he has slipped into a wheel rut again, and Huifang colours. Refusing to be seated above the rest, the guests of honour take places by age at three tables, waited on by Jin Shufang, Li Yulin, Lin Chunxi, Yuan Baozhu, Wang Lanbao, Lu Sulan, Su Huifang, and Wang Guibao. As the wine goes round every heart lifts but Ziyu's: thinking of Qinyan ill behind closed green windows with the medicine stove burnt low, he pleads drunkenness and lies down on the couch. Xiao Cixian reports that he took the boy's pulse the day before — exhaustion compounded by grief, with blood already in the phlegm, and months of quiet needed — and Ziyu, listening, sinks further. Zhongqing proposes that the day leave some record; Cixian agrees to paint it, and the nine men compose a linked poem in order of age, opening on jade trees and dawn orioles and turning rhyme after rhyme. Nanxiang draws the crucial line — that the flower's heart lacks truth, and who can tell a male butterfly? — which fixes the difference between flowers and people; Wenze answers it neatly with mandarin ducks that are small but always paired. Gao Pin's coarse couplets are shouted down by the performers, and Wang Xun's line about stealing the moon goddess's elixir earns a rebuke of its own. Ziyu, barely present, writes only of eyes turning back in a glance — an allusion to Waking from a Dream that no one else understands — and is given the closing line, where snow alights on a blossoming branch and draws the whole poem together. Then they match each performer to a flower: Baozhu the peony, Huifang the herbaceous peony, Sulan the lotus, Yulin the emerald peach, Shufang the crab-apple, Lanbao the rose, Guibao the hibiscus, Chunxi the orchid. Ziyun says Qinyan's art is too fine and his temper too cold for anything but plum — which he loves besides — and asks to whom plum could belong if not to Yuxiang, at which Ziyu blushes; Qiguan is settled as apricot. Talk of yellow and white chrysanthemums leads Wenze to mention a new courtesan at the East Garden, brought from Guangxi by a lieutenant and then abandoned, known as White Chrysanthemum, whose history Gao Pin proceeds to supply in full. Before parting they fix the eighteenth for Yuan Baozhu's housewarming, Nanxiang boasting that he will stand a day's feast for every guest who fails to come. Huifang calls next day to give thanks, but Ziyu has fallen ill. On the eighteenth the company duly assembles from noon to midnight over forfeits, guessing games, and song; Zhang Zhongyu joins the eager crowd and Qiguan is well enough to appear, but Ziyu and Qinyan are both still unwell — so Nanxiang pays his forfeit and entertains them all again at his own house the next day.`,
  },
  18: {
    zh: `杜琴言病势缠绵，闭门谢客，只有同班几个相好时来宽慰；袁宝珠、陆素兰传来梅子玉那日的光景与"千万保重"的话，他听了反更伤感，怡园也久已屏迹。作者趁此写出梨园师傅一生四变：少年丰姿美秀，人所钟爱，名之为兔；二十以后相貌渐蠢而犹搔头弄姿、极意骚浪，名之为狐；三十以后嗓哑须出，唱不成戏，便买孩子教一年半载打发出去赚钱，赚得多的当儿子看待，赚不来的朝打暮骂，出师时开口三千五千吊，钱一到手仍旧赶出门去，连旧衣也不给一件，若无老婆还要徒弟伴宿，凶恶更胜猛虎；及至时运退了，只在班里打旗跑龙套，便只好比作狗。琴言到京四个月，徐子云已在他身上花去白银数千，所以长庆待他分外殷勤——若病上一年半载，只怕也就变了脸。另一边，魏聘才进华府满一月，宾主竟不曾见面，几次请见皆以有事辞之；府中门客家人数百，豪奴架子极大，喜庆要派分子，撇兰聚赌、车夫厨子打抽丰，样样都得应酬，赔累已多，与顾月卿、张笑梅尚合式，与阎简安、王卿云则两下相厌，终日不过"两餐老米饭，一枕黑甜乡"。他出城探望，见子玉卧病未愈，又寻不着李元茂，遂转到伏虎桥张仲雨处。二人先去戏园听了几出陈腐旧戏，远远望见李元茂同孙嗣徽在对楼，仲雨只笑这对郎舅是天生一对废物。转上酒楼小酌，聘才诉说华府冷落，仲雨便替他讲一篇富贵场中的处世学：上等如陈眉公、李笠翁，以书画诗文哄得王侯动色，如今没有了；二等须有"一团和气、二等才情、三斤酒量、四季衣服、五声音律、六品官衔、七言诗句、八面张罗、九流通透、十分应酬"十样，句句还得添一个"不"字——和气不变、才情不露、酒量不醉、衣服不当、音律不错、官衔不做、诗句不荒、张罗不断、通透不短、应酬不俗；三等则只是"童"与"半通、会足恭、巴结内东、奴才拜弟兄、拉门面靠祖宗、钻头觅缝打抽风"。落到实处，他教聘才先打通华公子寸步不离的亲随林珊枝，再以小便宜笼络华光宿身边八龄班诸童，日日把好话吹进主人耳里，谓之"放线雀儿"；顺其性而留一分在后，谓之"收劣马"；不求一事而献些小忠小信使其放心，谓之"钓金蝉"；至于阎简安、王卿云一流不中用的人，应酬反被要紧人看轻，不如不睬。聘才听得心折，戏称要具门生帖拜他为师。归途上他远远跟着李元茂、孙嗣徽，到了东园——茅茨土墙的娼寮门口挤满闲人。两个脑满肠肥的妇人坐在长凳上招手，唱着"只要京钱二百文"的浪曲，又编出"一个儿脸麻，一个儿眼花，瞎眼鸡同着癞虾蟆"来骂那两人，聘才听懂了几乎失笑。继而帘内走出一个素净俊俏、鬓边插花的南方女子，捧过琵琶弹了一套《昭君怨》，又自弹自唱身世：从前是鸳与鸯，如今是参与商，千山万水追寻，只落得一枝残菊傲秋霜；石公坝伤心，画眉塘丧命，全湘沅湘、瓜州扬州、淮徐济兖一路北来，只不晓那薄幸儿郎在何处藏——声调凄惨，把聘才听得流下泪来。这便是高品所说从广西新来的白菊花。其时孙嗣徽的荷包已被剪绺割去，只好咬文嚼字地叹"君子无故，玉不去身"；李元茂也说起去年在戏园门口跌交被人扶起、银子随即不见的旧事，听者暗笑。原来白菊花是梧州人，嫁过一个不长进的姓宋的，后与湖广籍张姓营员誓同偕老，去年张营员奉差进京，她便带了盘费与一个小丫头追赶，误了路径出了广西，盘费用尽，又得了假信说他补了千总不得出京，遂卖衣搭船北上；及到京时，那人早已差竣回乡，她只得沦落卖笑。开门户的陶妈妈家中原有三个姑娘，最好的是扬州人玉天仙，近日接着广东巨富奚十一——此人家有千万，兄弟十人皆作道府，老太爷现任提台，家开洋行，正是京城人眼中的大冤桶；只是他一天要吃一二两鸦片，几个姑娘既吃不惯黑土烟，言语又不投机。陶妈妈便把同是广西人的白菊花接来认作义女，专为笼络他。奚十一一听是广西人便满心欢喜，步行而至，见她娇如花、柔如水，语音又相合，大为得意；她烧烟的手段极好，能挑得五寸长的面条烟供他一口吸尽。他一连宿了七八天，日费数十吊，做衣打首饰，连老鸨帮闲无不打把式，他也从不吝啬。菊花动了从良之念，奚十一本为物色小星而来，两下正合；老鸨舍不得这注财源，两头挑唆，说他家有几十房小星、恋新弃旧，又说菊花有本夫、性子不好，二人却全然不信。末了奚十一叫官媒作媒，赏了陶妈妈五十金，买丫头、雇老妈，将白菊花领回做了姨奶奶——她受尽狼狈之后，总算走上了一条自以为安稳的路。`,
    en: `Du Qinyan's illness drags on. He shuts his door and sees only a few friends from his own troupe; when Yuan Baozhu and Lu Sulan bring word of how Mei Ziyu looked that day and his plea that Qinyan take care of himself, the message deepens rather than soothes his grief, and the Yi Garden has long gone unvisited. Here the narrator anatomises the four transformations of a theatre master. In youth, lovely and desired, he is called a rabbit. After twenty, coarsening yet still simpering in fine clothes and playing the wanton on stage, he is a fox. After thirty, his voice gone and his beard grown, unable to perform, he buys children, trains them a year or so, and sends them out to earn — treating the profitable ones as sons and beating and cursing the rest, demanding three or five thousand strings at their graduation and then turning them out without even an old coat, and, if he has no wife, making them share his bed: for cruelty he outdoes a tiger. And when his luck fails at last and he is carrying flags among the extras, he can only be called a dog. Qinyan has been in the capital four months, and Xu Ziyun has already spent several thousand taels on him, which is why Changqing treats him so well — though a year's illness would doubtless change that face. Meanwhile Wei Pincai has completed a month in the Hua mansion without once meeting his employer, every request for an audience put off with pleas of business. Among several hundred retainers and servants the arrogant ones expect deference; every celebration means a subscription, and grooms, cooks, and hangers-on come round for their share, so that his outlay mounts steadily. He gets on with Gu Yueqing and Zhang Xiaomei, is cordially disliked by Yan Jian'an and Wang Qingyun, and passes his days on two meals of old rice and one good sleep. Going out of the city he finds Ziyu still ill and Li Yuanmao's door locked, and drifts on to Zhang Zhongyu at Fuhu Bridge. They sit through several stale operas — spotting Li Yuanmao and Sun Sihui across the gallery, whom Zhongyu dismisses as a matched pair of fools — then adjourn upstairs to drink, where Pincai pours out his disappointment. Zhongyu answers with a complete science of advancement. The first rank — men like Chen Meigong and Li Liweng, whose brush and verse made princes court them — no longer exists. The second requires ten things: an even temper, second-rate talent, three catties of wine, four seasons of clothes, five tones of music, a sixth-rank title, seven-character verse, address on all eight sides, knowledge of the nine schools, and complete sociability — each with a negative attached: the temper must not alter, the talent not show, the wine not intoxicate, the clothes not go to the pawnshop, the music not slip, the title not be taken up, the verse not run to seed, the address not falter, the knowledge not fall short, the sociability not turn vulgar. The third rank needs only to have sat the boy's examination and to be half-literate, obsequious, ready to flatter the master, to swear brotherhood with servants, to trade on ancestors and squeeze what it can. In practice, he says, Pincai must first win over Lin Shanzhi, who never leaves Hua Guangsu's side, and buy the boys' troupe with trifles, so that good words reach the master daily — that is flying the kite. Yield to a man's temper but always hold one part back — that is reining the vicious horse. Ask nothing, offer small proofs of loyalty until he trusts you — that is angling for the cicada. As for useless men like Yan Jian'an and Wang Qingyun, courting them only lowers you in the eyes of those who matter. Pincai is so impressed that he offers, in jest, to enrol as a formal disciple. Walking back he trails Li Yuanmao and Sun Sihui to the East Garden, where a crowd blocks the door of a thatched brothel. Two gross women on a bench beckon and sing a ditty promising heaven for two hundred cash, then improvise another about a pockmarked face and a pair of weak eyes, a blind cock and a warty toad — which Pincai alone understands to be aimed at his companions. Then a plainly dressed southern woman with a flower at her temple comes out, takes up a pipa, plays Zhaojun's Lament, and sings her own history: once mandarin ducks, now two stars that never meet; a thousand hills crossed for nothing; a single ravaged chrysanthemum braving the autumn frost; grief at one dam, near death at another, down all the rivers and through every checkpoint to the capital, with no notion where the faithless man has hidden. Her voice is so desolate that Pincai weeps. This is White Chrysanthemum, the newcomer from Guangxi that Gao Pin had described. Sun Sihui discovers his purse has been cut away and can only lament in classical tags that a gentleman does not part with his jade; Li Yuanmao recalls how he fell at a theatre door the year before, was helped up, and found his silver gone. Her story is this: born at Wuzhou, married to a worthless man named Song, she pledged herself to an officer surnamed Zhang from Huguang; when he was sent north last year she followed with a little money and one maid, missed the road out of Guangxi, ran out of funds, and was told falsely that he had been made a lieutenant and could not leave the capital, so she sold her clothes for passage — only to arrive and find him long since gone home. Madam Tao, who keeps the house, already has three girls, the best of them Yu Tianxian of Yangzhou, and has lately hooked Xi Shiyi — a Cantonese worth millions, with ten brothers in prefectural posts, a father commanding a province, and a trading house of his own, in short the fattest pigeon in Beijing. But he smokes one or two ounces of opium a day, the girls cannot manage the black paste, and the dialects do not match; so Madam Tao takes in White Chrysanthemum, a fellow Cantonese, as an adopted daughter expressly to hold him. Delighted to hear of a Guangxi woman, Xi Shiyi walks there on foot, finds her lovely as a flower and soft as water and her speech his own, and is enchanted; she rolls him pipes so skilfully that a five-inch thread of paste vanishes in a single breath. He stays seven or eight nights at some tens of strings a day, ordering clothes and jewellery and tipping everyone in the house without a murmur. She begins to think of leaving the trade, and since he came looking for a concubine the match suits both — only Madam Tao, loath to lose the income, works on each in turn, telling her that he keeps dozens of concubines and tires of each in a month, and telling him that the woman has a husband living and will never settle. Neither believes a word. In the end Xi Shiyi engages an official matchmaker, pays Madam Tao fifty taels, hires a maid and a nurse, and takes White Chrysanthemum home as his concubine — after so much hardship, a road she takes for safety.`,
  },
  19: {
    zh: `魏聘才照张仲雨的法门行事，先向林珊枝献尽殷勤，又在八龄班诸童身上赔尽辛苦。珊枝原是联锦部有名的小旦，后被华公子重价买进，八龄班皆其所教，音律最精；一日他教玉龄唱曲，恰是聘才最得意的一支，聘才在旁帮腔，五音不乱、唇齿分明，把珊枝惊倒，自此便在华光宿面前朝夕称赞。华公子最信珊枝，心上遂有了这个人。聘才又今日送扇明日送荷囊，把苏、扬来的小戏子哄得称心，对管总办事的更巴结周到，不到一月人人说好，连张笑梅、顾月卿也不及他的鞠躬尽瘁，阎简安、王卿云则更不必说。一日探得华公子出门，他便约张笑梅出城访冯子佩。子佩是早丧严亲的宦家子弟，母亲钟爱，任他游荡歌场，家业已渐萧条，书斋里残书笔砚、箫管琵琶、刀箭杂物堆得满桌；人却袅娜多姿，一见便与张笑梅搂抱厮闹。杨梅窗随后闯入，因是忌辰不能听戏，四人便上酒楼。席间议叫相公：聘才自恃已是华府中人，偏点杜琴言，杨梅窗点李玉林，张笑梅点蓉官——不料琴言、玉林皆称病不来，聘才只得改叫琪官，梅窗另叫二喜。二喜一到，听说聘才如今住在华公府，"哎呀"一声，态度顿改，京师人情随势转舵，于此可见。及问起玉林、琪官何以不来，二喜便讲出一段骇人的原委：广东奚十一在京候选，本带数万银子要捐大官，用去了只捐得知州；他初到京便看中登春班的春兰，每出戏做十几副行头，首饰皆金，师傅又拿话呕他赚他，出师又花五千吊，二人天天一炕吹烟一桌吃饭，一碗菜说咸了便连碗砸掉，几百吊的皮褂说风毛不好便顺手撕碎。既有春兰仍不肯放过别人，只要中意便一味硬来：他有个洋漆描金的木桶，口小底大，内藏机括，中层一块板上开两孔，外有铜锁；哄人俯身去捡桶中金银翡翠，两手一伸进去便被锁住，退不出、提不起，他便不问愿否强行施暴，事后知机的多讨些东西，不知机的与他争闹，反被诬为讹诈，送到坊里拘禁挫辱，还要师傅拿百十吊去赎。李玉林为人忠厚，被他一套衣裳一对镯子并赏师傅四十吊动了火，几番相请皆不遂意，末了灌了酒骗他看桶；玉林早听过风声不肯上前，他便仗力硬按，扯脱了裤子，亏得跟班硬闯进来才罢，玉林哭骂而出，他反倒把人送了坊。当天傍晚他又叫了琪官，琪官不知就里，两手果然伸进桶去；他正要如法炮制，被琪官一腿踢中要害，疼得蹲了下去，琪官又不顾疼痛用力乱扭，把机括扭坏，两手刮得血淋淋才脱身。琪官告知袁宝珠，宝珠气极进怡园禀了徐子云，子云大怒，连夜托人从坊里要出玉林，又要禀明巡城御史搜桶治罪；不知谁走漏了风声，奚十一既怕闹事又已银钱告罄，便在吏部报了"资斧不继"出京，据说躲往天津，只怕过些时还要回来。众人听罢又惊又快，冯子佩痛骂，杨梅窗却打趣问他是否见过那宝贝桶子，二喜也被聘才、梅窗你一句我一句地取笑，红着脸拧人。聘才心里暗想：不料琴言、琪官既唱了戏还这般有骨气。散后梅窗拉着子佩往胭脂巷玉天仙家去了。另一头，潘其观自被苏蕙芳灌醉哄骗之后，一直懊恼：既想收拾他又怕他交游广，淫心未断，又心疼那二百吊，还疑心张仲雨与蕙芳串通，唠唠叨叨说些影射话，把仲雨也得罪成了对头。他往保定查铺两月回来，与老婆闹了几场，无处出气，便独自一人不带仲雨，傍晚径到蕙芳门口，恰遇他从春航处归来。蕙芳吃了一惊，只得殷勤让进，先赔话解释那日是华公子传去实在短伺候；潘三却说"二百吊钱倒买张老二吐了我一脸酒"，又声称已戒了酒。蕙芳心知今日难脱，索性发狠留他，亲自到厨下备了三荤三素与一壶黄酒，说不喝酒也要应个景儿；潘三终究忍不住，一壶又添一壶，醉后故态复萌，抱住他脸上乱嗅，口口声声只要他"有一点心"，什么都依，连银号都肯给。蕙芳一面周旋，一面正色相责：我今年才十八，既已出师，求你留我一点脸；又反将他一军，说听张仲雨讲潘三爷最是大方，中意的人千儿八百都肯，怎么在我面上才花二百吊就要捞本。潘三无言可答，只好再以银钱笼络，许他随时到铺子里取钱。蕙芳瞧见他腕上一只十两重的金箍，便故意拿徐子云所赏的全绿翡翠镯子给他看，叹一声"可惜没个金的配他"，潘三心疼半晌，终究抬着膀子说"你要就给你罢"。镯子刚上手，他便拦腰抱住，抠摸不已，蕙芳无法可施，只得拿他衣襟上的小牙梳替他梳胡须敷衍。正在危急之际，忽听外面人声脚步，嚷道坊官查夜；潘三是个财主，最怕查三问四，慌得从黑影里一溜烟跑了出去。`,
    en: `Acting on Zhang Zhongyu's instructions, Wei Pincai lavishes attention on Lin Shanzhi and takes endless trouble over the boys' troupe. Shanzhi was once a noted dan of the Lianjin company before Hua Guangsu bought him at a great price; he trained the boys himself and knows music to the bone. One day, teaching Yuling an aria that happens to be Pincai's own favourite, he hears Pincai join in from the side — every tone true, every syllable distinct — and is astonished. From then on he praises the man morning and evening, and since Hua Guangsu trusts Shanzhi above all and knows he seldom praises anyone, Pincai secures a place in his master's mind. Fans one day and embroidered pouches the next win over the Suzhou and Yangzhou children, and such assiduous courtesy to stewards and clerks that within the month everyone speaks well of him — more warmly than of Zhang Xiaomei or Gu Yueqing, let alone Yan Jian'an and Wang Qingyun. Learning that Hua Guangsu is out, Pincai takes Zhang Xiaomei to call on Feng Zipei, a young gentleman who lost his father early, was indulged by his mother into a career of pleasure, and whose fortune is visibly thinning; his study is a heap of tattered books, brushes, flutes, a pipa, swords, and arrows, while he himself is so willowy that he and Zhang Xiaomei fall at once into an embrace. Yang Meichuang bursts in after them, and since the day is a memorial day when theatre is forbidden, the four go to a wine shop. Choosing performers, Pincai — trusting his new standing — sends for Du Qinyan, Meichuang for Li Yulin, Zhang Xiaomei for Rongguan; but Qinyan and Yulin both plead illness, so Pincai calls Qiguan instead and Meichuang calls Er Xi. Er Xi arrives, hears that Pincai now lives in the Hua mansion, and audibly changes his manner — the capital's regard turning with the wind. Asked why Yulin and Qiguan are absent, he tells a shocking story. Xi Shiyi came to the capital with tens of thousands to buy high office and got only a sub-prefecture; he fastened first on Chunlan of the Dengchun troupe, making a dozen sets of costumes for every role and gold ornaments besides, paying five thousand strings to buy out his apprenticeship, sharing pipe and table with him daily, smashing a dish for being too salty and tearing a fur coat worth hundreds of strings because the trim displeased him. Yet Chunlan did not stop him pursuing others by force. He keeps a lacquered and gilded barrel, narrow-mouthed and broad-based, fitted with a hidden catch: an inner shelf pierced with two holes and a lock outside. He invites a boy to reach in for the gold or jade he has thrown down, and when both hands are through, the catch closes; the barrel cannot be lifted or the hands withdrawn, and he does as he likes. Those with sense afterwards demand more presents; those who protest are accused of extortion and handed to the ward office, where friendly officers hold and humiliate them until a master pays a hundred strings to redeem them. Li Yulin, an honest soul, was courted with a suit of clothes, a pair of bracelets, and forty strings for his teacher; when repeated invitations got nowhere, Xi Shiyi plied him with wine and tried to show him the barrel. Yulin had heard the rumours and would not approach, so Xi Shiyi simply pinned him down and pulled off his trousers — and only let go when Yulin's attendant forced his way in. Yulin went out cursing and weeping, and Xi Shiyi had him committed to the ward office. That same evening he sent for Qiguan, who knew nothing and put both hands in; as Xi Shiyi moved on him, Qiguan kicked him squarely between the legs, and while he crouched in agony the boy wrenched at the mechanism until it broke, freeing his hands raw and bleeding. Qiguan told Yuan Baozhu, Baozhu went furious to the Yi Garden, and Xu Ziyun, hearing also of Yulin, sent that night to have him released and prepared to petition the city censor to seize the barrel and prosecute. Someone carried the warning; short of money and afraid of a case, Xi Shiyi pleaded exhausted travelling funds at the Board of Civil Office and left the capital, reportedly for Tianjin — though he will surely be back. The company is by turns appalled and delighted; Feng Zipei curses, Meichuang asks slyly whether he has inspected the famous barrel himself, and Er Xi, teased in his turn, reddens and pinches his tormentors. Pincai privately marvels that Qinyan and Qiguan, being what they are, should have such spine. Afterwards Meichuang carries Zipei off to Yu Tianxian's house in Rouge Lane. Elsewhere, Pan Qiguan has been brooding since Su Huifang drank him under the table: he wants revenge but fears the boy's connections, his appetite is undiminished, he grudges the two hundred strings, and he suspects Zhang Zhongyu of conspiring in the trick, dropping insinuations until Zhongyu becomes an enemy in earnest. After two months inspecting his cloth shops at Baoding and several quarrels with his wife, he arrives alone at Huifang's door one evening, just as Huifang returns from Chunhang's. Concealing his alarm, Huifang ushers him in and explains that the last time it was Young Master Hua who sent for him; Pan retorts that his two hundred strings bought only Zhang the Second's vomit in his face, and announces that he has given up wine. Seeing no escape, Huifang resolves to brazen it out, goes himself to the kitchen for six dishes and a pot of wine, and observes that even a reformed man must honour the occasion. Pan drinks one pot and then another, and drunk becomes himself again, clutching at Huifang and swearing that if the boy will only show him a little heart he shall have anything, a bank included. Huifang parries and then answers gravely: he is eighteen and out of his apprenticeship, and asks only to be left a little face. Then he turns the argument round — Zhang Zhongyu says Pan Third is open-handed, good for a thousand on anyone he fancies, so why, having spent two hundred here, is he already trying to recover his stake? Pan has no reply and falls back on money, inviting him to draw on the shop whenever he likes. Noticing a ten-ounce gold band on Pan's wrist, Huifang produces the flawless green jade bracelet Xu Ziyun gave him and sighs that it wants a gold one to match; Pan winces, hesitates, and finally holds out his arm. The bracelet is barely on before he seizes Huifang round the waist, and Huifang, out of stratagems, takes the little ivory comb from Pan's lapel and busies himself combing the man's beard. At that moment voices and footsteps sound outside with a cry that the ward officer is making his night round — and Pan Qiguan, a rich man with a horror of questions, bolts through the dark and out of the gate.`,
  },
  20: {
    zh: `原来蕙芳下厨房时便已算定潘其观今日不肯罢休，预先叫家人假扮坊官、又请了两个坊卒，到歪缠不清时嚷将进来；果然色大胆小的潘三中计而逃。虽赚得一只金镯，蕙芳仍恐他明日再来，只好到田春航寓中躲避几天。潘三一路丧气而归，因惧内不敢公然在外胡闹，只打算慢慢再拿银钱买转他的心。这边梅子玉自怡园一别，与杜琴言再未会面：琴言的病时好时发，不进园子唱戏，偶尔力疾到怡园一走；子玉的病本由忧闷而起，到怡园偏值琴言不来，到秋水堂又逢师傅请客或有人坐着，又不便再去寻陆素兰，只得怅恨缘悭。琴言在家并不知子玉来过几次，又听说他害病，愈觉悲酸，却因从未到过梅宅不便自去。作者点明症结：长庆只认得钱多，不认得人好——子玉是逢场作戏的读书人，在琴言身上从未花过一个钱，长庆自然不肯拉拢。此时高品应河间知府之聘去修志，刘文泽被岳母接了同往直隶总督衙门，旧日名士已少了三人，只剩徐子云、萧次贤、史南湘、颜仲清、田春航、王恂六人往来。五月初一恰是王文辉生日，家中亲友毕集，颜夫人等女眷也去了，王恂与仲清便脱不开身；子云、次贤去拜寿时约了南湘、春航同回怡园看龙舟、赏榴花。四人换了便服乘马入园，沿峭壁转上青石羊肠小道，古藤碍首、香草钩衣，又顺围墙经过一带红阑修竹，登三十余级石磴到缥缈亭，见几十株苍松黛色参天，树杪微露碧瓦朱楼。春航叹其文心之妙不让庐山香炉峰，南湘品为幽邃而不险仄，子云说这都是萧次贤手笔，布置时数易其稿。转下石磴便是停云叙雨之斋，壁上一联"拜石有时具袍笏，看云无处不神仙"，署款竟是华光宿；南湘失惊于其词翰，子云说他天分极高只是工夫稍浅，为势位所误，春航则说单看此联便可见其胸次之雅、意气之豪。正说着，水榭那边荡出两只花艇：王兰保掖起罗衫、盘了辫发，鬓边倒插一枝榴花，手持紫竹篙一面撑一面赶鸭，惊得鸳鸯乱飞，白鹭掠檐而去，次贤笑说这才真是"打鸭惊鸳鸯"。船中走出袁宝珠、金漱芳、苏蕙芳、陆素兰四人，连兰保共是五枝名花。众人登舟游赏，遥望桃坞、杏村、梨院、牡丹香国与梅子青黄的梅崦；素兰细说桂岭、兰径、菊畦、稻庄与鹤栏鹿棚的所在。及至吟秋水榭，三层楼阁建在水中：第一层十二间作六面样式，面面玻璃雕窗，隔作阴阳明暗十二处；第二层四面式样，望见柳阴中泊着三丈多高的龙舟，舟身彩画青龙，五六层架子上尽是绫锦伞盖旗幡、孔雀珍珠银针诸伞，剪彩装成楼台庭院，人物皆以走线行动，顶上立一只扎成的金毛孔雀，二十四人荡桨，内设八音班奏粗细十番——这是次贤在江苏见过后画出图样选匠制造的；第三层三面式样，一色香楠木，地高气爽，凭阑可收怡园八九分全景。众人下楼入席，宾主四人与名花五人依齿而坐。龙舟盘旋阁前，家人放起五千一串的百子爆竹；忽有四个短衫赤膊、红巾扎额的健仆上前叩头，鼓响一声齐翻筋斗入水，原来是夺标：家人抛下白鸭与红漆葫芦，四人或俯或仰、或沉或浮，游龙戏水般争夺，看得满座欢笑。次贤便以此设令：取园中所结的漆葫芦，各人抽一根《水浒传》人物酒筹，注上名号，把筹封进葫芦抛入水中，捞着谁的便由谁行令。于是次贤得"李逵大闹浔阳江"，南湘得"武松醉夺快活林"而连胜三拳又打了通关，子云得"宋江怒杀阎婆惜"，笑问座中谁是阎婆惜，素兰红了脸把嘴一呶推给宝珠，宝珠反笑他上当——筹上不饮的才是阎婆惜。轮到蕙芳偏是"潘金莲雪天戏叔"，要露胸搭肩、以残酒相劝、末了作怒容自饮，并坐左邻正是春航；他先错叫了宝珠一声"叔叔"，笑得宝珠喷了一桌，众人逼迫不过，只得勉强行完，兰保还打趣说这是捉弄姓潘的报应。兰保得"鲁智深醉打山门"，漱芳得"金翠莲酒楼卖唱"须弹琵琶敬酒，春航得"一丈青捉王短虎"要与蕙芳牵红巾饮三个交杯，臊得蕙芳直说难星过完；不料宝珠得的是"王婆楼上说风情"，须以扇打西门庆、又要西门庆跪捧莲钩敬酒，恰恰轮着子云屈了一膝，南湘一句玩笑更引得漱芳笑闪了腰；末一根是素兰的"梁山泊群雄聚义"，合席各饮三杯收令。酒到红日沉西方才吃饭。饭后南湘提议趁晚霞去看小赤城的榴花，众人复坐船过红桥，从神仙洞穿出杏楼、桃坞，但见一二百株榴花回绕如城，红霞闪烁、流火欲燃，间以黄白玛瑙诸色。天晚将别，子云说园中各处对联尚须添设几副，今日被龙舟耽误，改日再请一游，并约梅子玉、颜仲清同来；南湘、春航应允而去，五位名旦也各自散了。`,
    en: `It emerges that while Huifang was in the kitchen he had already reckoned that Pan Qiguan would not be shaken off, and had arranged for his servants to impersonate a ward officer, with two constables hired for the part, to raise the alarm at the critical moment. Timid as he is lecherous, Pan Qiguan duly fled. The gold bracelet is won, but Huifang, expecting him back, goes to hide for some days at Tian Chunhang's lodging; Pan, too afraid of his wife to make a public scandal, resolves to buy the boy's goodwill by degrees instead. Since the evening at the Yi Garden, Mei Ziyu and Du Qinyan have not met once. Qinyan's illness comes and goes, he no longer performs, and only occasionally drags himself to the garden; Ziyu's own sickness began in melancholy, and whenever he goes to the garden Qinyan is absent, while at the Autumn Water Hall he finds the teacher entertaining or strangers sitting about — and he cannot keep calling on Lu Sulan. Qinyan, for his part, never learns how often Ziyu has come, hears only that he is ill, and cannot decently present himself at the Mei house. The narrator names the obstruction plainly: Changqing understands money, not merit, and since this young scholar has never spent a cash on Qinyan, the teacher sees no reason to help. The circle has thinned, too — Gao Pin has gone to compile a gazetteer for the prefect of Hejian, and Liu Wenze has been carried off by his mother-in-law to the governor-general's office in Zhili — leaving Xu Ziyun, Xiao Cixian, Shi Nanxiang, Yan Zhongqing, Tian Chunhang, and Wang Xun. The first day of the fifth month is Wang Wenhui's birthday, and with the house full of relations and Lady Yan among the women guests, Wang Xun and Zhongqing cannot get away; so Ziyun and Cixian, having paid their respects, carry off Nanxiang and Chunhang to the Yi Garden for the dragon boats and the pomegranates. The four change into plain clothes and ride, climbing a goat-track of blue stone where old vines catch at their hats, past red railings and bamboo, then up thirty-odd steps to the Pavilion of the Misty Void, where dozens of ancient pines rise black-green and a corner of tiled roof shows above the treetops. Chunhang says the artistry rivals the Incense Burner Peak of Mount Lu; Nanxiang calls it deep without being vertiginous; Ziyun says it is all Xiao Cixian's work, redrafted many times. Below stands the Studio of Pausing Clouds and Narrating Rain, hung with a couplet on saluting stones in court dress and finding immortals wherever clouds are watched — signed by Hua Guangsu. Nanxiang is startled by the calligraphy; Ziyun says the man's natural gifts are very high and only his application shallow, spoiled by rank and wealth; Chunhang replies that the couplet alone reveals an elegant mind and a bold spirit. As they speak, two flower-boats swing out from the water pavilion: Wang Lanbao, gown tucked up and braid coiled, a pomegranate blossom stuck at his temple, poles along and chivvies the ducks until the mandarin ducks scatter and a white egret skims the eaves — which Cixian calls beating the ducks and startling the lovebirds. Out of the cabins come Yuan Baozhu, Jin Shufang, Su Huifang, and Lu Sulan, five famous flowers counting Lanbao. They board and drift, looking out at the peach slope, the apricot village, the pear court, the peony kingdom, and the plum slope where the fruit hangs half green and half yellow, while Sulan describes the cassia ridge, the orchid path, the chrysanthemum field, the rice farm, and the crane and deer pens. The Chanting-Autumn Water Pavilion stands in the lake in three storeys: the first of twelve rooms on a hexagonal plan, every face glazed and carved, divided into twelve light and shadowed compartments; the second four-sided, from which they see the dragon boat moored under the willows, thirty feet high, its hull painted as a green dragon and its five or six tiers loaded with brocade canopies, banners, and parasols of peacock feather, pearl, and silver needle, with cut-silk palaces and temples whose figures move on strings, a gilt peacock at the summit, twenty-four rowers, and a band playing within — all built from drawings Cixian made after what he had seen in Jiangsu; the third three-sided and panelled in fragrant nanmu, high enough that eight or nine parts of the whole garden lie open to view. They descend to table, four hosts and guests with five performers seated by age. The dragon boat wheels before the hall, five thousand firecrackers are let off in a string, and four grooms in short jackets and red headbands come up, kowtow, and at a drumbeat somersault backwards into the lake — for the prize-taking. Ducks and lacquered gourds are thrown in, and the four twist and dive after them, to the delight of the table. Cixian turns this into the game: gourds grown in the garden are opened, each guest draws a Water Margin tally and marks it with his name, the tallies are sealed inside and thrown into the lake, and whoever's gourd is retrieved must perform his forfeit. Cixian draws Li Kui making havoc at Xunyang; Nanxiang draws Wu Song seizing Happy Grove, wins three bouts running, and drinks a full circuit besides; Ziyun draws Song Jiang killing Yan Poxi and asks who at the table is Yan Poxi, at which Sulan blushes and passes it to Baozhu, who laughs that Sulan has caught himself out, since the tally says Yan Poxi is the one who does not drink. Huifang's tally is Pan Jinlian teasing her brother-in-law in the snow, requiring an open collar, a hand on the shoulder, wine offered from his own lips, and a final cup drunk in feigned anger — with Chunhang seated on his left. He first calls Baozhu "brother-in-law" by mistake, making him spray the table, and only completes the forfeit under general coercion, while Lanbao observes that this is what comes of tricking a certain Pan. Lanbao draws Lu Zhishen drunk at the monastery gate; Shufang draws Jin Cuilian singing in the wine shop and must play the pipa; Chunhang draws the Ten-Foot Green capturing Dwarf Tiger Wang and has to drink three cross-cups with Huifang under a knotted kerchief, until Huifang declares his run of bad luck over — only for Baozhu to draw Dame Wang telling of romance upstairs, which requires him to strike Ximen Qing with a fan and to be offered wine by a kneeling Ximen Qing holding his slipper, a part that falls to Ziyun, who bends a knee while Nanxiang's joke makes Shufang laugh until he wrenches his back. The last gourd is Sulan's, the heroes swearing brotherhood at Liangshan, and the whole table drinks three cups to close. They eat as the sun goes down. Afterwards Nanxiang proposes seeing the pomegranates at Little Red Wall by the evening light, so they take the boat back over the red bridge and out through the grotto past the apricot tower and peach slope, where one or two hundred pomegranate trees ring the ground like a wall of red cloud, shot through with yellow, white, and agate. As they part, Ziyun says several pavilions still want couplets, that the dragon boat has consumed the day, and that he will invite them again — with Mei Ziyu and Yan Zhongqing — to finish the task. Nanxiang and Chunhang agree and drive off, and the five performers disperse.`,
  },
};
